19. THE DOUBLE-CLAIM PROBLEM

This problem is different from Bitcoin's double-spend.

In Bitcoin:

double-spend = attempting to spend the same monetary unit more than once.

In this architecture:

double-claim = attempting to use the same industrial event to support two incompatible claims.

Example:

EVENT E123

One statement claims:

E123 → PRODUCT A

Another claims:

E123 → PRODUCT B

Both may contain correctly signed documents.

The problem is not necessarily the signature.

The problem is:

«Which of the two claims can legitimately consume that state?»

---

20. INDUSTRIAL STATE CONSUMPTION

A conceptual solution is to treat certain industrial states as consumable objects within the external protocol.

When a state has been validly assigned to a claim:

that state should not be legitimately reusable as though it were still available.

This does not make the object a Bitcoin UTXO.

Industrial UTXO should be understood only as an architectural analogy.

The central idea is:

«A consumed state cannot be presented again as a free state without generating a verifiable conflict.»

This makes the Double-Claim Problem an explicit protocol property.

