30. HETEROGENEOUS WORKLOADS

This presents an important challenge.

Not all industrial work has the same nature.

It may involve:

- CPU computation;
- GPU computation;
- ASIC computation;
- ZK proof generation;
- simulation;
- cryptographic validation;
- sensor validation;
- cross-source validation;
- data transformation;
- specialized industrial computation.

Therefore, the protocol should not automatically assume that everything can be measured as:

hashrate.

The more general concept is:

«Measured Computational Contribution»

Each Job type can define its own verifiable unit of contribution.

The research challenge is to create a reasonable economic normalization across heterogeneous workloads.

---

31. THE REWARD FOLLOWS THE WORK

The central economic thesis is:

«The reward follows the work, not the intermediary.»

This does not mean eliminating intermediaries.

An intermediary can provide verifiable value through:

- industrial integration;
- infrastructure;
- financing;
- storage;
- insurance;
- support;
- compliance;
- interfaces;
- operations;
- auditing;
- maintenance.

Therefore:

«Intermediaries may earn for verifiable value added.»

The proposed economic rule is:

«A fee should correspond to verifiable value added.»

---

32. INCENTIVES

The system attempts to connect three magnitudes:

NEED → WORK → VALUE

Industry needs something verified.

That requirement creates a Job.

The Job creates work.

The work creates a verifiable result.

The result creates value.

The value funds the reward.

Thus:

Industry creates demand.
Demand creates Jobs.
Jobs create work.
Work creates evidence.
Evidence creates verifiable results.
Results create value.
Value pays work.

